---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Hanfu vs Qipao: Traditional Wear Comparisons"
  description="Compare Hanfu vs Qipao, Chinese vs Japanese vs Korean traditional clothing. Side-by-side style, history, and travel experience comparisons for visitors."
  canonicalUrl="/compare"
  articleDate="2026-08-03"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "Style Comparisons", url: "/compare/" }]}>
  <section class="pt-24 pb-16">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <span class="text-xs font-medium text-[#b5343a] tracking-[0.15em] uppercase">ChinaStyle Travel Guide</span>
      <h1 class="font-serif-sc text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold leading-tight tracking-tight text-stone-900 mt-3">
        Chinese Traditional Wear: Style Comparisons
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-stone-500 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        Not sure which style fits your trip? Here''s how the major Chinese traditional clothing types compare — and how they stack up against Japanese and Korean traditional wear.
      </p>

      <!-- Hanfu vs Qipao -->
      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-12 mb-4">Hanfu vs Qipao</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
        <div>
          <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-stone-100 aspect-[4/5] mb-4">
            <img src="/images/cat-hanfu.webp" alt="Hanfu: flowing robes, crossed collars" class="w-full h-full object-cover transition-transform duration-500 hover:scale-105" loading="lazy" width="1536" height="1024" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-stone-100 aspect-[4/5] mb-4">
            <img src="/images/cat-qipao.webp" alt="Qipao: form-fitting, mandarin collar" class="w-full h-full object-cover transition-transform duration-500 hover:scale-105" loading="lazy" width="1536" height="1024" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Hanfu is loose, layered, and flowing — think wide sleeves and floor-length robes dating back 3,000+ years. Qipao is form-fitting, single-piece, with a high mandarin collar and side slits, emerging from 1920s Shanghai. Hanfu best suits outdoor historical sites (Xi''an city walls, gardens); qipao suits urban evening settings (the Bund, cocktail events, fine dining). Hanfu rental: 100-300 RMB. Custom qipao: 300-800 RMB at South Bund Fabric Market. <a href="/hanfu-vs-qipao" class="text-[#b5343a] hover:underline font-medium">Read the full Hanfu vs Qipao comparison →</a></p>

      <!-- Hanfu vs Tang Suit -->
      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-12 mb-4">Hanfu vs Tang Suit</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
        <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-stone-100 aspect-[4/5]">
          <img src="/images/dyn-han.webp" alt="Hanfu: layered, dramatic, floor-length" class="w-full h-full object-cover transition-transform duration-500 hover:scale-105" loading="lazy" width="1536" height="1024" />
        </div>
        <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-stone-100 aspect-[4/5]">
          <img src="/images/cat-tang-suit.webp" alt="Tang Suit: jacket, frog buttons, practical" class="w-full h-full object-cover transition-transform duration-500 hover:scale-105" loading="lazy" width="1536" height="1024" />
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Hanfu is an entire outfit with multiple garment layers. Tang suit is primarily a jacket worn over your own clothes — far more practical for quick changes during a busy travel day. Hanfu photoshoots take 30-60 minutes of preparation. Tang suit experiences take 10-15 minutes. For travelers short on time but wanting a cultural fashion experience, Tang suit workshops in Beijing hutongs are the most efficient option. Cost: 50-150 RMB for Tang suit rental; 200-500 RMB for a custom-made jacket.</p>

      <!-- China vs Japan vs Korea -->
      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-12 mb-4">Chinese vs Japanese vs Korean Traditional Wear</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
        <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-stone-100 aspect-[3/4]">
          <img src="/images/dyn-tang.webp" alt="Hanfu (China) — flowing, layered" class="w-full h-full object-cover transition-transform duration-500 hover:scale-105" loading="lazy" width="1536" height="1024" />
        </div>
        <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-stone-100 aspect-[3/4]">
          <img src="/images/dyn-song.webp" alt="Kimono (Japan) — wrapped, obi belt" class="w-full h-full object-cover transition-transform duration-500 hover:scale-105" loading="lazy" width="1536" height="1024" />
        </div>
        <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-stone-100 aspect-[3/4]">
          <img src="/images/dyn-ming.webp" alt="Hanbok (Korea) — short jacket, full skirt" class="w-full h-full object-cover transition-transform duration-500 hover:scale-105" loading="lazy" width="1536" height="1024" />
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed mt-4">Chinese Hanfu uses cross-collars (right-over-left), wide sleeves, and flowing silhouettes with sashes. Japanese kimono wraps left-over-right with a broad obi belt creating a straight, tubular silhouette. Korean hanbok features a short jacket (jeogori) with a high-waisted, voluminous skirt (chima) creating an A-line shape. All three share silk as a primary fabric, but each evolved distinct silhouettes reflecting their culture''s aesthetic values: Chinese fluidity, Japanese structure, Korean volume. For travelers visiting multiple East Asian countries: Hanfu photoshoots in Xi''an are generally the most affordable (100-300 RMB vs 3,000-10,000 JPY for kimono in Kyoto vs 50,000-150,000 KRW for hanbok in Seoul).</p>

      
      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-12 mb-4">Quick-Reference Comparison Table</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b-2 border-stone-200 text-left">
              <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-stone-800">Style</th>
              <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-stone-800">Silhouette</th>
              <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-stone-800">Best For</th>
              <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-stone-800">Prep Time</th>
              <th class="py-3 font-semibold text-stone-800">Cost (RMB)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-stone-600">
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-stone-800">Hanfu</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Loose, layered, flowing robes with wide sleeves</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Historical site photoshoots (Xi''an, Luoyang)</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">30-45 min</td>
              <td class="py-3">100-500</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-stone-800">Qipao</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Form-fitting one-piece, high collar, side slits</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Evening events, urban settings (Shanghai)</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">10-15 min</td>
              <td class="py-3">100-800 (custom)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-stone-800">Tang Suit</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Straight jacket, mandarin collar, frog buttons</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Festivals, casual wear, souvenirs (Beijing)</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">10-15 min</td>
              <td class="py-3">50-500 (custom)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-stone-800">Zhongshan Suit</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Tailored jacket, four pockets, turned-down collar</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Formal occasions, wearable souvenir (Nanjing)</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">10 min (off-rack)</td>
              <td class="py-3">200-1,200 (custom)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-stone-100">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-stone-800">Ethnic Minority</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Varies: Miao silver+embroidery, Bai tie-dye, Tibetan chuba</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">Cultural immersion (Guizhou, Yunnan)</td>
              <td class="py-3 pr-4">15-30 min</td>
              <td class="py-3">50-200</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-12 mb-4">Which Style Should You Choose?</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Choose Hanfu if you want the most dramatic, photogenic experience and have 1-2 hours to spare. Choose qipao if you want something elegant for an evening out or a custom-made souvenir. Choose Tang suit if you want something quick, comfortable, and wearable beyond the photoshoot. Choose ethnic minority clothing if you want an off-the-beaten-path cultural deep-dive in Guizhou or Yunnan. Many travelers combine two styles across different cities: Hanfu in Xi''an, then custom qipao in Shanghai.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

      <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-stone-200 text-center">
        <h3 class="font-serif-sc text-lg font-semibold text-stone-900 mb-3">Sources & Further Reading</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1 text-sm text-stone-500 inline-block text-left">
          <li>&bull; <a href="https://en.chinaculture.org/" class="text-[#b5343a] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">China Culture.org</a> — Ministry of Culture official resource on Chinese heritage and traditions</li>
          <li>&bull; <a href="https://www.chinasilkmuseum.com/" class="text-[#b5343a] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">China National Silk Museum</a> — world’s largest silk and textile museum, Hangzhou</li>
          <li>&bull; <a href="https://en.dpm.org.cn/" class="text-[#b5343a] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Palace Museum</a> — original imperial robes and textile collections, Beijing</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

</BaseLayout>
